Expected elements: present

Provenance banner (badge, `aztec` slug, producer sentences), identity bar with chain-targeted exit, truncated hash, `Partial` chip, both stepping directions, scrubber, phase rail, NOIR tag; Code pane positioned at line 32; Call Trace/Event Log as one tabbed region with Call Trace open and seven frames; Values as a separate pane below with content; Transaction pane. Nothing forbidden: no spinner, no empty pane, no prose band above the panes, no full-width pane row, Values not a tab.

In my lens this reads as a competent dense tool that has been *lightened* rather than *designed for light*. The dark theme's elevation steps survive the flip as differences of a few percent against white, so page background, pane header bands, pane bodies and the provenance banner all occupy one lightness plateau and every pane boundary is carried by a hairline alone — clearest at the VALUES/CALL TRACE seam and the CODE pane's left edge.

The role system is the other half. "Changed" is green in the Code pane (`damage: 0→2000`) and amber in Values (`9000`, `2000`), while amber simultaneously means the `Partial` trace status. "Current" is cyan for the code line and the selected frame but dark-grey fill for iteration 3 of 8. Blue splits three ways across the identity bar (meter fill, FETCHING chip, links). The stepping cluster renders at two ink levels with nothing else distinguishing them, so "disabled while loading" is unreadable.

Highest-priority fixes: give the light theme real surface steps, and make one hue mean "changed" in both panes.

Rating: 6/10

